Title:
Pointing a Cursor at evading detection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Sophos CTU analyzes a threat actor’s AI-assisted workflow for developing and testing EDR evasion inside a “red team” post-exploitation framework.  
- The actor used an AI-native IDE (Cursor) and multiple LLM agents (e.g., Claude Opus) to orchestrate lab setup, technique ingestion from public research, iterative payload generation, and test/report cycles across VMs running Sophos, CrowdStrike, and Windows Defender.  
- The framework included Cobalt Strike malleable profiles, a Telegram Bot API external C2 channel, a Cloudflare Worker redirector/front, and Python tooling to inject shellcode into legitimate Windows executables while preserving functionality.  
- A central Python “loader generator” wrapped raw payloads (often Rust/Go) with layered encryption, evasion, and alternate execution methods, with ~80 modules covering 70+ techniques; Sophos notes claimed high bypass success but questions the supporting evidence.  
- The post highlights that AI primarily accelerated engineering and experimentation rather than providing autonomous reasoning, and links the activity to ransomware/data theft operations.  
- Useful for defenders tracking modern post-exploitation tradecraft and for red teamers understanding how adversaries are operationalizing AI-driven test harnesses.

Technical Focus:
- AI agent orchestration for offensive R&D (Cursor, Claude, MCP)
- EDR evasion testing pipelines and iterative payload refinement
- Malleable C2 / traffic shaping (Cobalt Strike profiles)
- External C2 via legitimate platforms (Telegram Bot API)
- Redirectors/fronting infrastructure (Cloudflare Workers)
- Windows payload loading/injection with functionality preservation

Use Cases:
- Build detections for Telegram-based C2 and Cloudflare Worker redirector patterns
- Threat hunting for AI-assisted dev artifacts (test directories, Git repos, VM lab traces)
- Modeling adversary EDR-evasion experimentation workflows in purple-team exercises
- Hardening controls around post-exploitation frameworks (Sliver/Cobalt Strike) and AD discovery tooling

Keywords:
Sophos CTU, EDR evasion, Cursor IDE, Claude Opus, AI agents, Model Context Protocol (MCP), Cobalt Strike, malleable C2, Sliver, Telegram Bot API, external C2, Cloudflare Worker, redirector, payload loader, shellcode injection, Rust payloads, Go payloads, Windows Server 2022, Active Directory discovery, MITRE ATT&CK mapping